it can be difficult to differentiate  between a food allergy and food  intolerance and with holiday meals  coming up again right around the corner  it's best to know what foods you need to  stay away from we connected with a food  intolerance expert on symptoms reaction  times  [Music]  Dr Jill Hart is a biochemist and a food  intolerance expert at York test she says  the clearest way to tell the difference  between a food allergy and an  intolerance symptoms and reaction time  allergies are the immediate reaction to  a food like swelling of the mouth  diarrhea or vomiting food intolerance or  sensitivities can take up to two days to  appear and those symptoms are very  wide-ranging so you might get anything  from IBS Sirius  digestive problems bloating  through to skin symptoms migraines  headaches and even things like low  energy and low mood the most common food  allergies are to milk eggs shellfish and  sesame allergies tend to be developed  through childhood or adulthood and at  that point are lifelong but food  intolerance can develop at any age and  it's not necessarily permanent we can  actually find out from that small blood  sample exactly what people are reacting  to York test is the UK's market leader  in food sensitivity testing it has hubs  in the United States the test identifies  up to 200 different foods in anyone over  the age of two in a recent study the  company found 80 percent of the people  who took a simple blood test felt better  after removing the food identified in  the test  Dr Hart says after you remove the  problem food from your diet give it two  to three months to get your gut health  back in check and then you can try to  reintroduce it back a little bit at a  time to see if you still react to those  Foods
 

